WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.230
How much to cook, and how to run production day.

2
00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:10.710
This is the video for module two of Catering Basics and Food Safety: menus, portions and production day.

3
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:18.290
A good event menu is a set of dishes that suit the guests, survive the journey and stay safe for hours.

4
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:23.640
Running out of food is embarrassing. Cooking far too much wastes money.

5
00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:29.620
And most catering disasters come from no buffer time, not from bad cooking.

6
00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.200
This video shows you how to plan all three.

7
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:51.750
First, ask the serving time, format, guest numbers, budget and any allergies. Then plan one plate: a starch, a protein, a side, and sometimes an extra. Choose dishes that hold well in a hot cooler, like jollof rice, stews, moi moi and well-cooked meats.

8
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:08.590
Fried plantain softens, salad wilts once dressed, and pounded yam forms a skin. Plan these for last-minute work, or swap them out. Halima dropped fried plantain from a wedding menu because it would be soft after a 3-hour hold.

9
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:21.340
Step one. Test your portion. Cook a measured amount of raw rice, serve it with your usual spoon into your usual pack, and count the packs. Your spoon and your pack decide your number.

10
00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.050
Step two. Multiply each portion by the number of guests.

11
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:34.420
Step three. Add a buffer, usually around 10 percent, because guest numbers grow and servers give bigger spoons.

12
00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:43.530
Step four. Turn totals into what you actually buy: kilograms of rice, whole birds, crates of eggs, litres of oil.

13
00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:54.990
Ngozi tested her portions: 120 grams of raw rice and one chicken piece per adult. For 100 guests, that is 12 kilograms of rice.

14
00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.620
She adds 10%, which gives 13.2 kilograms, so she buys 14.

15
00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:16.690
For chicken, 100 pieces plus 10% is 110. She cuts each bird into 8 pieces, and 110 divided by 8 is 13.75, so she buys 14 birds.

16
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:24.110
Coleslaw is 80 grams each, so 8 kilograms plus 10%, about 8.8 kilograms.

17
00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:36.040
To scale a recipe, divide the new servings by the old. From 10 servings to 50, the factor is 5. Season gradually and taste, and never cram a pot beyond its size.

18
00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:54.770
Ronke caters 100 packs for a 1:00 pm church anniversary, 45 minutes away. She works backwards from serving time. The day before, she steams moi moi and cools it fast, makes the stew base and refrigerates it, and shreds the cabbage.

19
00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:02.780
At 6:30 am, she boils chicken, starts the first batch of rice, and heats the stew base to boiling.

20
00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:12.770
By 10:00 am everything is cooked. She probe-checks the chicken at 70 degrees or above and the rice above 60.

21
00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:21.660
She packs hot food into coolers preheated with hot water for 10 minutes, and coleslaw into a separate cooler with ice packs.

22
00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:31.250
She leaves at 11:00 am and arrives at 11:45, with 75 minutes of buffer before serving.

23
00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:38.240
Set up a raw zone, with its own table, board, knife and bin for raw chicken, meat and fish.

24
00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:42.930
A cooking zone, with clean spoons that never touch raw meat.

25
00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:50.040
And a ready-to-eat zone for coleslaw and packing, wiped and sanitised, away from raw work.

26
00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:59.460
Wash your hands every time you move from the raw zone to any other zone. And never put raw items in the same box as ready food.

27
00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:06.560
Keep gas cylinders upright, outside or in a well-ventilated space, away from the flame.

28
00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:11.560
Check hoses and joints with soapy water. Bubbles mean a leak.

29
00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:20.490
If you smell gas, turn off the cylinder, do not switch lights or phones on or off nearby, and open doors and windows.

30
00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:36.170
To remember. Plan one plate, then multiply. Portion times guests, plus about 10%. Build your timetable backwards with buffers. Keep three zones. And preheat your coolers, with cold food in its own cooler.

31
00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:53.240
Now practise. Write two menu packages with holding notes. Build a quantity sheet for 50 and 100 guests. Plan a production day working back from a 1:00 pm serving, and sketch your kitchen zones with an equipment checklist.

32
00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:55.540
Continue with the lessons below.
